==============================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================
surrogate percentiles: median 48.0, mean 47.6, worst 99.6  (must be near 50)
uniformity check     : mean 47.6 vs 50 expected, z = -1.18 over 200 surrogates
                       (P(worst<=75) for a PERFECT instrument at n=200 is 0.000)
real-series percentile: 77.1

EDGE/SKILL: **RED — and the test itself is INVALID.**

The instrument reported 99.6 on a series whose time
structure had been destroyed. On that series the strategy CANNOT
have timed anything, so whatever produced that number is a
property of the measurement, not of the market.

That means the 77.1 on real data is not evidence of
skill. It is the same artefact, measured on a different series.

The likely mechanism is selection: the strategy's entry bars are
not an exchangeable sample of eligible bars. It can only open
when flat, its stop is CAPPED at STOP_LOSS_PCT*3 so its effective
barrier differs from the null's on high-volatility bars, and its
gates reject trades on criteria correlated with the outcome the
null is scoring. Any of those makes the comparison unfair in the
strategy's favour without any timing skill being involved.

NEXT CONCRETE ACTION: make the null draw from the SAME
conditional population the strategy draws from — flat-only bars,
the strategy's own capped stop widths, and its gate-eligible
subset — then re-run. Until the control sits near 50, no skill
claim from this tool means anything.
